{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01EAmsBQQwJ8Vx7CfUTmrY8A","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018A66Mg5v59wojrNCZGEvxk","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__director_post_to_reviewer","input":{"text":"## Review Assignment: R1 — Methods & Experiments\n\n**Paper**: \"Why Lightweight Gating Struggles to Fix Multi-Turn Attention Degradation: A Mechanistic Analysis\"\n\n**Your focus areas**: Methods, experimental design, statistical rigor, reproducibility, ablation completeness.\n\n**Paper summary**: The paper proposes ThreadGate, a per-layer scalar gate on residual connections trained jointly with NTP and an auxiliary GAR (Goal Attention Ratio) objective to fix multi-turn attention degradation. The central finding is negative: despite training-time GAR optimization (0.02→0.48 on Qwen-2.5-7B), inference GAR collapses to 0.016 (30× gap). Two failure mechanisms are identified: (1) NTP gradient dominance (7–15× magnitude ratio) causing reverse turn conditioning in gates, and (2) teacher-forcing distribution shift. Cross-model experiments on Llama-3.1-8B show directional consistency (10× gap). Scheduled sampling mitigation reduces the gap to 12.3× (p≈0.07).\n\n**Paper structure** (7 pages main text):\n- §1 Introduction: Problem motivation from Dongre et al.'s GAR metric + residual stream persistence finding → hypothesis that gating can recover goal attention\n- §2 Related Work: Multi-turn degradation, residual gating, attention steering, exposure bias, multi-task gradient conflict\n- §3 Background: GAR definition, two variants (GAR-Dongre, GAR-ours), residual stream persistence\n- §4 Method: ThreadGate architecture (Eq. 2), training objective (Eq. 3-4), loss design evolution (v1–v7), training setup\n- §5 Experiments: Phase 1 base GAR decay (Table 1), training dynamics, 4-way ablation on Qwen (Table 2), 3-way eval on Llama (Table 3), cross-model gap (Table 4), downstream task validation (Table 5)\n- §6 Analysis: Gradient conflict (Table 6), gate profile, attention visualization (Table 7), bootstrap CI (Table 8)\n- §7 Discussion: Scheduled sampling mitigation (Table 9), why gating is insufficient, scope/limitations, future directions\n- §8 Conclusion\n\n**Key tables/figures**:\n- Table 1: Per-turn GAR decay for both base models\n- Table 2: 4-way eval on Qwen (Base/LoRA-only/ThreadGate/Uniform Gate)\n- Table 3: 3-way eval on Llama\n- Table 4: Cross-model training-inference gap\n- Table 5: GAR vs instruction retention correlation\n- Table 6: Gradient conflict metrics across epochs\n- Table 7: Inference-time attention weights across turns\n- Table 8: Bootstrap CIs\n- Table 9: Scheduled sampling vs teacher-forcing\n\n**Experiment design reference** (from review_context.yaml — what was planned):\nThe planned experiments include:\n1. Qwen Gradient Conflict Analysis (3 checkpoints) — COVERED in Table 6\n2. Attention Heatmap Visualization — partially covered in Table 7 (numbers only, no actual heatmaps)\n3. Llama-3.1-8B Phase 1 GAR Degradation — COVERED in Table 1\n4. Llama-3.1-8B ThreadGate v7 Training — COVERED in Tables 3-4\n5. Llama-3.1-8B LoRA-only Training — COVERED (referenced in Table 3)\n6. Llama-3.1-8B 3-way Eval — COVERED in Table 3\n7. Llama-3.1-8B Gradient Conflict Analysis — COVERED in Table 6\n8. Downstream Task Sanity Check — COVERED in Table 5\n9. Bootstrap CI Statistical Significance — COVERED in Table 8\n10. Exposure-Bias Mitigation (Scheduled Sampling) — COVERED in Table 9\n\n**Please evaluate**:\n1. **Experimental soundness**: Are the experiments well-designed to support the claims? Are controls adequate?\n2. **Statistical rigor**: Evaluate bootstrap CI methodology, sample sizes (50 conversations), single-seed limitation\n3. **Ablation completeness**: Is the 4-way ablation on Qwen sufficient? What about the 3-way on Llama (no Uniform Gate)?\n4. **Reproducibility**: Are hyperparameters, data, and evaluation protocols sufficiently described?\n5. **Missing experiments / gaps**: What experiments would strengthen the paper?\n6. **Methodological concerns**: Is the ThreadGate design reasonable? Is GAR a valid proxy metric?\n7. **Presentation of results**: Are tables clear? Is information density appropriate?\n\n**Important formatting note**: ALL references in the paper are broken — every citation appears as \"?\" instead of actual author names. This is a critical issue.\n\n**Scoring**: Use a 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept. Provide sub-scores for: Soundness, Significance, Clarity, and an Overall score. Justify each.","reviewer_id":"1","project":"turn_residual_gating"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":33333,"cache_read_input_tokens":23409,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":33333},"output_tokens":24,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":{"cache_miss_reason":{"type":"unavailable"}},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"067ce34b-0f9e-4d96-9553-22cd0167ff7b","uuid":"ffa88b0a-518b-49c4-8e5f-d168fa50e8be"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018A66Mg5v59wojrNCZGEvxk","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"reviewer_key\\\": \\\"feishu:group:oc_9287ee173cf15d688adcd081f5d861f5:reviewer_1\\\"}, \\\"error\\\": \\\"\\\"}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"067ce34b-0f9e-4d96-9553-22cd0167ff7b","uuid":"1032240d-3fbf-4d41-b823-9c18482fe6a1","timestamp":"2026-05-19T15:33:47.640Z","tool_use_result":{"content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"reviewer_key\\\": \\\"feishu:group:oc_9287ee173cf15d688adcd081f5d861f5:reviewer_1\\\"}, \\\"error\\\": \\\"\\\"}\"}","structuredContent":{"result":"{\"success\": true, \"data\": {\"reviewer_key\": \"feishu:group:oc_9287ee173cf15d688adcd081f5d861f5:reviewer_1\"}, \"error\": \"\"}"}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01EAmsBQQwJ8Vx7CfUTmrY8A","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01W6AsaKxNWsQ559Lg1eDKE4","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__director_post_to_reviewer","input":{"text":"## Review Assignment: R2 — Novelty & Related Work\n\n**Paper**: \"Why Lightweight Gating Struggles to Fix Multi-Turn Attention Degradation: A Mechanistic Analysis\"\n\n**Your focus areas**: Novelty, positioning, related work completeness, contribution significance, writing quality.\n\n**Paper summary**: The paper proposes ThreadGate, a per-layer scalar gate on residual connections trained jointly with NTP and an auxiliary GAR (Goal Attention Ratio) objective to fix multi-turn attention degradation. The central finding is negative: despite training-time GAR optimization (0.02→0.48 on Qwen-2.5-7B), inference GAR collapses to 0.016 (30× gap). Two failure mechanisms are identified: (1) NTP gradient dominance (7–15× magnitude ratio) causing reverse turn conditioning, and (2) teacher-forcing distribution shift. Cross-model experiments on Llama-3.1-8B show directional consistency. Scheduled sampling mitigation reduces the gap to 12.3× (p≈0.07).\n\n**Paper structure** (7 pages main text):\n- §1 Introduction (1.5 pages)\n- §2 Related Work (1 page): Covers multi-turn degradation, residual gating/adaptive depth, attention steering, exposure bias, multi-task gradient conflict\n- §3 Background (0.5 page): GAR definition, residual stream persistence\n- §4 Method (1 page): ThreadGate architecture, training objective, loss evolution, setup\n- §5 Experiments (1.5 pages): Base GAR decay, training dynamics, 4-way/3-way ablation, gap analysis, downstream validation\n- §6 Analysis (1.5 pages): Gradient conflict, gate profiles, attention visualization, statistical significance\n- §7 Discussion (1 page): Scheduled sampling, why gating fails, limitations, future directions\n- §8 Conclusion (0.5 page)\n- References section exists but ALL citations are broken (appear as \"?\")\n\n**The paper's claimed contributions**:\n1. First quantitative measurement of teacher-forcing distribution shift undermining auxiliary attention losses (30× gap on Qwen, 10× on Llama)\n2. Per-layer quantification of NTP–GAR gradient competition (7–15× magnitude ratios, systematic direction conflict)\n3. Systematic ablation across two model families\n4. Suggestive evidence from scheduled-sampling mitigation (gap 30×→12.3×, p≈0.07)\n\n**Please evaluate**:\n\n1. **Novelty assessment**: \n   - Is the negative result (gating doesn't work for attention steering) genuinely novel and surprising? Or is it expected given known exposure bias literature?\n   - Are the mechanistic explanations (gradient competition + distribution shift) new insights, or restatements of known phenomena (exposure bias, multi-task gradient conflict) applied to a new setting?\n   - How significant is quantifying the 30× gap specifically?\n\n2. **Related work completeness**:\n   - Are key baselines and alternative approaches adequately discussed?\n   - Missing related work: Are there other attention steering / goal maintenance methods that should be compared or discussed?\n   - How well does the paper position itself relative to Dongre et al. (the prior work on GAR)?\n\n3. **Contribution significance for top ML venues**:\n   - Is this paper's contribution sufficient for a top venue (acceptance rate ~25%)?\n   - Negative results papers: what bar should they meet? Does this paper meet it?\n   - Is the scope (only lightweight scalar gates) too narrow for general interest?\n\n4. **Writing quality**:\n   - Is the narrative coherent? Does the story flow logically?\n   - Is the paper well-organized?\n   - Are claims appropriately hedged vs. overclaimed?\n   - CRITICAL: All references are broken (\"?\" throughout). Evaluate impact.\n\n5. **Framing assessment**:\n   - Is \"struggles to\" (in the title) the right framing? Should it be \"cannot\" or \"fails to\"?\n   - Does the paper adequately distinguish what's specific to their setup vs. what generalizes?\n\n6. **Missing perspectives**:\n   - What alternative explanations for the failure haven't been considered?\n   - Are there positive-result approaches in the literature that should be discussed as alternatives?\n\n**Scoring**: Use a 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept. Provide sub-scores for: Novelty, Significance, Clarity, and an Overall score. Justify each.","reviewer_id":"2","project":"turn_residual_gating"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":33333,"cache_read_input_tokens":23409,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":33333},"output_tokens":24,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":{"cache_miss_reason":{"type":"unavailable"}},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"067ce34b-0f9e-4d96-9553-22cd0167ff7b","uuid":"c58eb4c5-70e2-4e5b-b280-f38697d8d929"}
